  "slug": "pauli-noise-27c9",
  "summary": "New preprints analyze how coherent, correlated, and structured noise degrade gates and QEC, and how twirling or protocol design restores Pauli-like behavior.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the treatment of non-ideal noise structure in quantum hardware. Two preprints address converting coherent errors to stochastic Pauli noise: single-realization Pauli twirling reduces error-accumulation variance from Θ(L²) to O(L) in structured two-qubit circuits, and a generalized Bloch-vector analysis shows randomized compiling yields near-isotropic contraction. Correlated and structured noise is examined directly: a general average-gate-infidelity expression under spatiotemporally correlated noise finds CZ and sqrt(iSWAP) more resilient, and circuit-QED experiments show structured environmental noise makes photon loss Fock-state dependent, producing non-Markovian transients relevant to binomial codes. Complementary hardware-specific work proposes measurement-free QEC for single-photon loss in cavities, reset-assisted control via noisy mediators, parallel transversal CZ gates in trapped ions, a CCZ gate beyond strong Rydberg blockade, and confidence-aware Yb tweezer readout at half the imaging time. A Fe2+:forsterite qubit proposal is peripheral.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
